course: biology
lesson: 167
title: Australia's forests and Tasmania: devils, cassowaries, lyrebirds and cube-shaped droppings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Along Australia's wetter east coast and on the island of Tasmania there are rainforests and tall eucalyptus forests full of animals as odd as any desert creature.
explain: In the cartoon Looney Tunes, Taz the Tasmanian devil spins like a tornado and eats everything; the real devil is the size of a small dog, does not spin, but does screech and squabble loudly over a dead wallaby.
explain: Imagine a bird in the garden that copies your mum's phone ringtone, a car alarm and a camera click: that is a lyrebird, and other forest birds decorate their homes with blue bottle caps to impress a mate.
explain: There are also kangaroos that climb trees, gliders that sail fifty metres in the dark, and the wombat, which leaves cube-shaped droppings on rocks like little dice.
fact: Tasmanian devils are the biggest living meat-eating marsupials and scavenge as well as hunt.
fact: A contagious (catching) face cancer has killed many devils, and scientists keep healthy groups of devils safe to save the species.
fact: The cassowary is a big flightless bird with a helmet on its head and dagger-like claws; it can kick very hard, so people are told to keep away and never feed it.
fact: Male lyrebirds are famous mimics that copy the calls of many birds and sometimes human sounds such as camera shutters, car alarms and chainsaws.
fact: Male satin bowerbirds build a stick bower and decorate it with blue things such as flowers, feathers and bottle caps, and some bowerbirds arrange objects to change how big they look from the female's viewpoint.
fact: Tree kangaroos live in rainforests, climb with strong arms and can leap many metres down to the ground without getting hurt.
fact: Quokkas are small wallabies that live mostly on Rottnest Island and look like they are smiling, and touching or feeding them is against the rules and can be fined.
fact: Koalas sleep up to about twenty hours a day, and they are marsupials, not bears, even though people call them koala bears.
fact: Wombats are the only animals known to make cube-shaped droppings, which do not roll away, and they leave them on rocks and logs to mark their area.
fact: The thylacine, or Tasmanian tiger, was a striped meat-eating marsupial that looked like a dog; the last known one died in a zoo in 1936, and despite sightings no proof of any living since has been found.
fact: Sugar gliders are tiny marsupials with skin flaps between their legs that can glide about fifty metres from tree to tree at night.
fact: Tasmania's mountain ash trees are among the tallest flowering trees in the world, and the one called the Centurion is about a hundred metres tall, as tall as a thirty-storey building.
exercise: What kind of animal is the Tasmanian devil? | The biggest living meat-eating marsupial | A small kind of bear that lives only in Tasmania | A wild cat | A kind of wolf that came over on ships
exercise: How does a Tasmanian devil get its food? | It scavenges dead animals and also hunts | It eats only fruit | It spins like a tornado and swallows trees | It catches fish in the sea
exercise: What has killed many Tasmanian devils? | A contagious face cancer | Heavy snow storms every winter | Too much hot sunshine in summer | Attacks by hungry cassowaries
exercise: Why are people told never to feed a cassowary? | It can kick very hard with dagger-like claws | It is too shy and will starve without your food | It only eats insects, so it cannot hurt you | It will follow you home and sleep there
exercise: What does a cassowary have on its head? | A helmet | A crest of long feathers | Two short horns | A bright red frill
exercise: What is a male lyrebird famous for? | Copying other birds and even human sounds | Building the biggest nest of any bird in the forest | Flying the fastest | Swimming a long way under water
exercise: What does a male satin bowerbird decorate his bower with? | Blue things such as feathers and bottle caps | Red berries only, which he eats later in the winter | Snail shells only | Nothing, he keeps his bower plain and bare
exercise: Why does a bowerbird decorate his bower? | To impress a female | To keep out the rain | To store food for winter | To scare off the snakes
exercise: How do tree kangaroos get around their rainforest? | They climb with strong arms and leap down to the ground | They glide from tree to tree on flaps of skin like possums | They swim along rivers | They never leave the ground at all, even to sleep
exercise: Where do quokkas mostly live? | On Rottnest Island | In the Sahara | In Tasmania's mountains | In Antarctica
exercise: What can happen if you feed or touch a quokka? | You can be fined | You win a prize | It turns into a kangaroo | Nothing at all
exercise: How long can a koala sleep in a day? | Up to about twenty hours | About two hours | Never more than one hour | Exactly eight hours
exercise: What kind of animal is a koala? | A marsupial | A small bear | A monkey that lives in trees | A kind of rodent
exercise: What is special about wombat droppings? | They are cube-shaped | They glow in the dark | They smell of sweet roses | They are perfectly round
exercise: Why do wombats leave droppings on rocks and logs? | To mark their area | To feed the trees | To hide from foxes | To keep warm
exercise: What was the thylacine? | A striped dog-like marsupial that is now extinct | A real tiger that still lives wild in Tasmania today | A kind of striped bird | A big cat that swims across rivers
exercise: How far can a sugar glider glide? | About fifty metres | About fifty centimetres | About five kilometres | It cannot glide at all
exercise: How tall is the mountain ash called the Centurion? | About a hundred metres | About ten metres | About a kilometre or even more | About thirty metres
test: In Looney Tunes, Taz the Tasmanian devil spins like a tornado. What is the real devil like? | A dog-sized marsupial that screeches and squabbles over food | A spinning, tornado-shaped animal that eats anything | A huge bear that eats trees | A kind of devil with horns and a pointed tail
test: «koala bear's my fave bear» What is right? | Koalas are marsupials, not bears | Yes, they are the smallest kind of bear | Yes, they are panda cousins | No, koalas are a kind of monkey
test: «heard a chainsaw in the forest but no one was there, creepy» What could it be? | A lyrebird copying the sound | A wombat digging its burrow | A cassowary kicking a tree trunk hard | A quokka laughing
test: «3 koalas n 2 wombats, how many ears» | 10 | 5 | 15 | 20
test: «can i hug the quokka, it's smiling at me» What is right? | No, touching or feeding quokkas is against the rules | Yes, the smile means it wants a hug from you | Yes, but only if you give it a few chips first, then it's fine | No, it will bite your nose off
test: What would happen if wombat droppings were round instead of cubes? | They could roll off the rocks and logs where the wombat marks its area | They would turn into stones | Nothing, because wombats never leave droppings anywhere at all | They would grow into trees
test: «my nan says she saw a Tasmanian tiger last week» What is the best answer? | People still report them, but no proof of a living one has been found since 1936 | Yes, they are common in every garden | Yes, there is a whole zoo of them | No, they were always made up
test: «a big blue-headed bird just walked out of the trees, gonna give it my sandwich» What is right? | No, it is a cassowary, keep away and never feed it | Yes, cassowaries make friendly, gentle pets for kids | Yes, they love bread, so throw it a few slices | No, it is just a big chicken
test: «bowerbird's nest full of blue bottle tops lol, is he a thief» What is right? | He collects blue things to decorate his bower and impress a female | He is building a little bottle shop to sell things | He eats bottle tops to help him digest | He is hiding from the rain
test: «4 sugar gliders, no wait 3, how many tails» | 3 | 4 | 6 | 12
test: Why might keeping healthy devils in a safe group help save the species? | The face cancer cannot spread to them there | They can learn to spin there | They get much bigger and much stronger on the island | They turn into thylacines there
exam: Identify the principal characteristic of the droppings of the wombat. | They are cubic in shape and are deposited upon rocks and logs to mark territory | They are spherical and are buried beneath the soil | They are luminous at night | They are deposited exclusively in water, in the manner of the platypus
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the koala. | It is a marsupial and in no sense a member of the bear family | It is the smallest member of the bear family | It is closely related to the giant panda of China and eats bamboo | It is a species of monkey
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the condition of the Tasmanian devil population? | It has been greatly reduced by a contagious facial cancer | It has expanded across the sea to the mainland of Asia | It is unaffected by any disease | It has been entirely replaced by the thylacine
exam: Ascertain the faculty for which the male lyrebird is chiefly renowned. | The imitation of the calls of other birds and of human sounds | The construction of bowers decorated with blue objects | Gliding between trees by means of membranes of skin | Breaking through sea ice with its head
